We've successfully deployed a pod named httpd-app-t1q3. We require some data to be copied from the jump-host to this specific Pod. Further details are outlined below:


Copy file /home/thor/welcome.txt to pod httpd-app-t1q3 Pod under location /opt.

Note: The kubectl utility on jump-host has been configured to work with the kubernetes cluster.

Pod 'https-app-t1q3' exists

Pod is in 'Running' state

File 'welcome.txt' copied to pod 'httpd-app-t1q3'

Solution
Step 1: Verify the Pod is running
bash
kubectl get pod httpd-app-t1q3
Expected output:

text
NAME              READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
httpd-app-t1q3    1/1     Running   0          1m
Step 2: Verify the source file exists on the jump-host
bash
ls -l /home/thor/welcome.txt
Step 3: Copy the file into the Pod using kubectl cp
The syntax is kubectl cp <source> <namespace>/<pod>:<destination>. Since no namespace is specified, we assume default:

bash
kubectl cp /home/thor/welcome.txt httpd-app-t1q3:/opt/welcome.txt
If the Pod is in a specific namespace (other than default), use:

bash
kubectl cp /home/thor/welcome.txt <namespace>/httpd-app-t1q3:/opt/welcome.txt
Step 4: Verify the file was copied into the Pod
bash
kubectl exec httpd-app-t1q3 -- ls -l /opt/welcome.txt
Expected output:

text
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root ... /opt/welcome.txt
You can also print the file content to confirm:

bash
kubectl exec httpd-app-t1q3 -- cat /opt/welcome.txt
Requirements checklist
Requirement	Status
Pod httpd-app-t1q3 exists	✅ Verified
Pod is in Running state	✅ Verified
File /home/thor/welcome.txt copied to /opt in the Pod	✅ Copied via kubectl cp
Notes / troubleshooting
tar: not found error: kubectl cp relies on tar inside the container. If the image (e.g., a minimal httpd/alpine-based image) doesn't have tar, the copy will fail with a message like exec: "tar": executable file not found. In that case, use an alternative:

bash
kubectl exec -i httpd-app-t1q3 -- sh -c 'cat > /opt/welcome.txt' < /home/thor/welcome.txt
/opt doesn't exist or isn't writable: If /opt is missing or read-only, create it first or choose a writable path:

bash
kubectl exec httpd-app-t1q3 -- mkdir -p /opt
Permission denied: If the container runs as a non-root user and /opt is root-owned, either exec as root (if allowed) or copy to a writable location like /tmp and move it.

Namespace: If the pod isn't in default, always prefix the pod name with the namespace in kubectl cp: <namespace>/httpd-app-t1q3:/opt/welcome.txt.
